      summary: "Two days after David returns to Ziklag from defeating the Amalekites, a young man arrives from Saul's camp with torn clothes and earth on his head, bearing Saul's crown and armlet. He claims that he found the wounded king leaning on his spear on Mount Gilboa and killed him at his own request — a story that flatly embellishes the account of Saul's suicide. David and his men mourn and fast until evening for Saul, for Jonathan, and for the fallen house of Israel. Then David has the messenger executed out of his own mouth, for daring to stretch forth his hand against the Lord's anointed. He laments Saul and Jonathan in the great elegy 'How are the mighty fallen' — the Song of the Bow, written in the book of Jasher — and commands that it be taught to the children of Judah.",
