      historianNote: "The Amalekite's tale contradicts 1 Samuel 31, where Saul falls on his own sword; he has evidently looted the body and invented a mercy-killing, expecting a reward from Saul's rival — a fatal misreading of David. The crown (nezer) and armlet he carries were genuine royal insignia across the ancient Near East; armlets appear as royal regalia in Egyptian and Assyrian art. The book of Jasher ('the Upright'), also cited at Joshua 10:13, was a lost national anthology of Hebrew war poetry, and David's lament belongs to a well-attested ancient genre of royal elegy — its refusal to mention any hope of reward, and its praise of the very king who hunted David, mark it as one of the oldest and most authentic pieces of poetry preserved in the Bible.",
