      "historianNote": "The narrator's note that Beeroth 'was reckoned to Benjamin' and that its people had fled to Gittaim is doing quiet work. Beeroth was one of the four Gibeonite towns that tricked Joshua into a covenant of protection (Joshua 9:17), and 2 Samuel 21:1-2 reveals that Saul had tried to exterminate the Gibeonites in violation of that oath. If the Beerothites fled Saul's purge, then Rechab and Baanah — Benjaminite in enrollment, Gibeonite in blood — may have nursed a vendetta against Saul's house that they now dress up as the LORD's vengeance and David's interest. The parenthesis about Mephibosheth (4:4) is likewise structural, not stray: with Ish-bosheth dead, readers must know that Saul's line survives only in a lame child, no threat to anyone — which makes David's later kindness to him (2 Samuel 9) grace rather than politics, and makes the assassins' 'service' as useless as it is wicked.",
