      "historianNote": "The tsinnor by which Joab's men entered (5:8; 1 Chronicles 11:6 names Joab) is usually connected with the Warren's Shaft system, discovered in 1867 — a tunnel from inside the city toward the Gihon spring — though excavation since the 1990s has shown the vertical shaft itself was likely not used for drawing water until later, so the invaders may rather have followed the spring's tunnels or the word may mean a conduit or even a grappling approach; the debate is genuinely open. What is not in doubt is the site: the narrow Ophel ridge south of the Temple Mount, where the massive Stepped Stone Structure — possibly the biblical Millo, 'the filling' — terraced the eastern slope in exactly this period. Jerusalem was old (Execration Texts, Amarna letters) but small, and it was neutral: a border city belonging to neither Judah nor the northern tribes, a capital chosen the way new federal capitals have been chosen ever since. The Tel Dan stele's ninth-century Aramaic phrase bytdwd, 'House of David,' shows the dynasty founded here was known to Israel's enemies as a fact of history within about a century and a half.",
