      "historianNote": "The new cart is the tragedy's seed. Numbers 4 assigns the ark to the Kohathite Levites, to be covered by priests and carried on staves upon men's shoulders — the sons of Kohath were given no wagons 'because the service of the sanctuary belonging unto them was that they should bear upon their shoulders' (Numbers 7:9), and touching the holy things meant death (Numbers 4:15). A cart is how the Philistines returned the ark (1 Samuel 6), acceptable ignorance in pagans; Israel copying the Philistine method is another matter, and 1 Chronicles 15:13 has David draw exactly this conclusion: 'we sought him not after the due order.' The second procession, with bearers and sacrifices, follows the Torah's order. Uzzah's instinct to steady the ark feels innocent to modern readers, but the narrative's point is that the ark is not furniture to be managed — the same lesson Beth-shemesh learned (1 Samuel 6:19). Obed-edom 'the Gittite' is probably a Levite of Gath-rimmon (cf. 1 Chronicles 26:4-5) rather than a Philistine of Gath, though the ambiguity has charmed commentators for centuries.",
