      "summary": "The king sits in his cedar house, at rest from his enemies, and is troubled by the disproportion: 'I dwell in an house of cedar, but the ark of God dwelleth within curtains.' Nathan the prophet, making his first appearance, blesses the plan — and that night the word of the LORD overrules them both. God has walked in tent and tabernacle since Egypt and never asked for cedar; it is not David who will build the LORD a house, but the LORD who will make David one. The oracle gathers up his whole story — taken from the sheepcote, kept through every danger — and then opens the future: a seed after him whose kingdom God will establish, who shall build the house, whose throne shall stand for ever; a son whom God will chasten when he sins but never abandon as He abandoned Saul: 'my mercy shall not depart away from him.' David goes in and sits before the LORD, and his prayer — 'Who am I, O Lord GOD? and what is my house?' — accepts the reversal entirely: he asks for nothing except that God do as He has said. The covenant made in this chapter carries the rest of the Bible on its back.",
