      "historianNote": "In the ancient Near East, temple-building was the signature act of a secure king: Gudea of Lagash, Hammurabi, and the Assyrian monarchs all advertise the sanctuaries they raised, and a god's temple crowned a dynasty's legitimacy. Chapter 7 knowingly inverts the pattern — the deity declines the temple and grants the dynasty instead, deferring the building to the son (the stated reason, per 1 Chronicles 22:8, that David was a man of blood). Scholars have long compared the oracle's form to the royal grant, a covenant of sheer favor bestowed for loyal service, unlike the conditional suzerainty treaties behind Sinai: sin brings chastening 'with the rod of men,' not annulment. The Hebrew turns on one word, bayit — house as palace, house as temple, house as dynasty — a triple pun no translation fully carries. This 'sure mercies of David' becomes the taproot of messianic hope (Psalm 89; Isaiah 9 and 11; Ezekiel 34), and the New Testament opens by tying its subject to it: 'the son of David.'",
