      "historianNote": "The word driving the chapter is hesed — covenant loyalty — and David names it 'the kindness of God,' the language of sworn treaty: he is discharging the oath of 1 Samuel 20:14-17, where Jonathan begged exactly this for his house. In the ancient Near East the normal fate of a fallen dynasty's heirs was extermination (Baasha and Zimri in Kings do precisely that), which is why Mephibosheth falls on his face; a permanent seat at the royal table was the era's instrument of honor and custody at once. The closest parallel is Jehoiachin of Judah, 'who did eat bread continually' at the Babylonian court (2 Kings 25:29) — and cuneiform ration tablets excavated from Nebuchadnezzar's Babylon actually list oil for 'Yaukin, king of the land of Yahud.' Lo-debar, east of the Jordan near Mahanaim, lay in the same Gileadite refuge country that had sheltered Ish-bosheth; its name can be punned in Hebrew as 'no word' or 'no pasture' — a nowhere place, from which grace fetches him.",
