      "historianNote": "In the ancient Near East the beard was the visible sign of a free adult male; Assyrian reliefs render kings' beards in elaborate oiled curls, and to shave a man — as Isaiah 7:20 threatens with the 'hired razor' — was to unman him publicly. Cutting the garments to expose the buttocks was the standard degradation of prisoners of war (Isaiah 20:4 describes exactly this), so Hanun has treated ambassadors, whose persons were sacrosanct under every ancient diplomatic convention from Mari to Amarna, as captives. The hired coalition is historically telling: the tenth century was precisely the era when Aramean states — Zobah under Hadadezer chief among them — were consolidating in Syria, and mercenary service for silver (1 Chronicles 19:6 prices it at a thousand talents) was their export. Joab's mutual-support arrangement against a double envelopment is one of the oldest clearly described pieces of field tactics in Scripture, and his closing sentence is a soldier's theology: do your utmost, and leave the outcome to the LORD.",
