      "historianNote": "The opening line is a dated indictment: spring, after the rains, was the ancient campaigning season — Assyrian annals arrange whole reigns by yearly spring campaigns — and the narrator notes that the king who should be at Rabbah is on his roof. The verbs of verse 4 carry the power asymmetry: David 'sent... took... she came' — a subject summoned by an absolute monarch had little standing to refuse. Uriah is a Hittite, yet he keeps Israel's war-camp discipline (soldiers on campaign were consecrated and abstained, Deuteronomy 23:9-14; compare 1 Samuel 21:5) more faithfully than Israel's king; Hittite military instructions and loyalty oaths from Boghazkoy show exactly this soldier's ethic of the shared hardship of the camp. The letter carried by its own victim has a famous parallel in the Iliad's Bellerophon, sent to Lycia bearing 'baneful signs' ordering his death — a motif ancient hearers knew, and shuddered at. The title of Psalm 51 reads this chapter as the psalm's occasion: 'when Nathan the prophet came unto him, after he had gone in to Bath-sheba.'",
