      "historianNote": "Court prophets across the ancient Near East existed to bless the crown: the Mari letters (eighteenth century BC) preserve dozens of oracles delivered to King Zimri-Lim, overwhelmingly reassuring, and Assyrian kings collected prophecies confirming their legitimacy. A prophet walking into the throne room to convict the king of capital crimes — adultery and murder both carried death under Israel's law, and no court could try the king — is nearly without parallel, and Nathan survives it by the juridical parable, a trap that makes the judge pass sentence on himself. David's 'fourfold' (the Torah's own penalty for a stolen sheep, Exodus 22:1) will be paid in sons: the infant, Amnon, Absalom, Adonijah. The mourning customs are pointedly inverted — fasting, lying on the earth, and washing afterward belonged after a death, not before — which is why the servants are baffled; David explains he was petitioning, not mourning. Rabbah's 'city of waters' was the lower town guarding the spring of the Jabbok headwaters below the citadel — Jabal al-Qal'a in modern Amman — whose seizure doomed the upper city.",
