      "historianNote": "A chariot with fifty outrunners was the recognized pomp of Near Eastern royalty — precisely what Samuel warned a king would take (1 Samuel 8:11), and the display Adonijah will copy (1 Kings 1:5). The city gate was Israel's courtroom, where elders sat and cases were heard (Ruth 4:1-11; Amos 5:15), so Absalom is filibustering the judiciary itself. Hebron was a masterstroke: David's first capital, Absalom's own birthplace (2 Samuel 3:2-3), and a city with reason to resent the court's removal to Jerusalem. The 'forty years' of 15:7 is an old textual knot — the Lucianic Septuagint, the Syriac, and Josephus all read 'four years,' which most scholars accept. The flight's topography is exact and walkable today: down from the City of David, across the Kidron ravine, up the Mount of Olives — the route later trodden by another weeping Son of David (Luke 19:37-41; John 18:1). Ahithophel of Giloh was plausibly Bathsheba's grandfather, if the Eliam of 2 Samuel 11:3 is Eliam the son of Ahithophel among the thirty (2 Samuel 23:34) — which would give his treason a very personal edge.",
