      "historianNote": "Sheba's cry is the exact slogan the northern tribes will raise, word for word, when they secede under Jeroboam (1 Kings 12:16) — the narrator is showing the crack that will one day split the kingdom. Abel-beth-maachah is securely identified with Tel Abil el-Qamh, a commanding mound above the Huleh Valley near the Lebanese border, under excavation since 2013 by Azusa Pacific University and the Hebrew University; its Iron Age I–IIA levels — including a cultic building and a tiny, exquisite faience head of a bearded elite male — confirm a substantial northern center in exactly this period. The woman's proverb, 'they shall surely ask counsel at Abel,' remembers the town as a seat of ancient arbitration, and she belongs to a recognized office: the 'wise woman,' like her counterpart from Tekoah (2 Samuel 14), could speak for a city and negotiate with generals. Her grim bargain — one head for a city — is siege-diplomacy attested across the ancient Near East.",
