      "historianNote": "The 'last words of David' belong to a genre the ancient Near East knew well: the royal testament, in which a dying king distills his reign into counsel — compare the Egyptian Instruction of Amenemhet or Assyrian royal apologia — though here the king speaks as prophet, and the ideal is not dynastic cunning but justice in the fear of God. The roll of the mighty men reads like an authentic archive: elite corps with fixed numbers ('the Three,' 'the Thirty') are attested in Egyptian and other ANE armies, and the list's mixed nationalities — Ammonite, Hittite, men of Maachah — fit a professional warband recruited in exile. Several names anchor the wider story: Asahel dead since chapter 2, Eliam the father of Bathsheba, Ahithophel's son. That the list ends with 'Uriah the Hittite' — thirty-seventh, unavenged, loyal — is the book's quietest and most devastating editorial judgment on its greatest king.",
