      "historianNote": "Census anxiety was ancient and real: Exodus 30:12 requires every counted man to pay a ransom 'that there be no plague among them when thou numberest them,' and at Mari on the Euphrates the census (tebibtum, 'purification') was a ritually fraught act that districts sometimes fled. Counting the people asserted royal ownership of what belonged to God — hence Joab's horror. The parallel account in 1 Chronicles 21 attributes the incitement to Satan where Samuel says 'the LORD,' one of Scripture's most discussed theological revisions, and calls the Jebusite 'Ornan'; the name Araunah may not be Semitic at all but a title — compare Hurrian ewri-ne, 'the lord' — perhaps the last ruler of Jebusite Jerusalem. A threshingfloor, a high, wind-swept, communal rock, was a natural sacred site across the Levant. 2 Chronicles 3:1 seals the geography: Solomon built the house of the LORD 'in mount Moriah... in the place that David had prepared in the threshingfloor of Ornan.' The book of Samuel ends on the temple's exact footprint.",
